<co-content>
 <p>
  There are two ways to use Claude Code:

1. You can buy a monthly subscription that includes Claude Code. If you take this route, there are limits on how much of Claude Code you can use on a given day / month.
 </p>
 <p>
  2. You can connect Claude Code to an API Key and pay for actual token usage costs.
 </p>
 <p>
  Know more about the pricing and plans
  <a href="https://claude.com/pricing" title="">
   here
  </a>
  .
 </p>
 <p>
  The first exercise in this class can consume $1.50 or more in tokens. I would assume that you can easily consume $50+ dollars in tokens if you complete all of the examples / exercises. You are not required to complete the exercises before the final assignment, but they are valuable learning experiences. I would suggest carefully tracking token costs and determining if a monthly subscription is more cost effective. Also, I would strongly encourage putting limits on your token usage (e.g., don't auto recharge, etc.) if you are not used to Claude Code costs.
 </p>
 <p>
  For me personally, the costs for Claude Code are far cheaper than doing the comparable work myself.
 </p>
 <p>
  For my personal use, I pay for a monthly subscription and
  <em>
   <strong>
    regularly
   </strong>
  </em>
  hit the token limits and have to take a break. This is a great time to think through design, process, etc. Hitting the token limit is a reminder for me to pause and do some reflection. For my work at Vanderbilt, we pay token costs.
 </p>
</co-content>
